+
+# Skill: Orchestration Lab and Iris-app Session Coherence Guard
+
+Run at the end of a work session, before commit or handoff. Check only this session's change.
+
+## Modes and safety
+- Follow the invocation's mode: plan, audit-only and discuss-first inspect and report without editing; build permits
+  scoped repairs.
+- Never commit or push unless asked. Never read, print or write secret values; inspect examples and key names instead.
+- Leave unrelated changes alone, and keep committed workflow logic out of vendor-specific agent folders.
+
+## Where things live
+- Authorised intent: this Scope's `SCOPE.md`, `scope.yaml`, and the decisions in `memory/` and `decisions/`; in
+  `~/pro/orchestrator`, the README's "Direction", `AGENTS.md` and `docs/GLOSSARY.md`. Steward: Justin (`scope.yaml`).
+  Open intent questions: `memory/central-scope.md`, "Waiting".
+- Current state and next steps: `STATE.md`. Read it first. Open work: GitHub issues on the map, orchestrator#140.
+- Decisions: `memory/` (Scope relations, this lab's processes, the working rules in force), `decisions/`, a design
+  report's opening in `reports/`, the orchestrator's `AGENTS.md` and `docs/GLOSSARY.md`. What broke: `FRICTION.md`.
+- Rules owned elsewhere: Justin's user-wide `~/AGENTS.md` and `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`; in `~/pro/orchestrator`,
+  `SECURITY-REVIEW.md`, `AGENTS.md` ("Astra review", "Versions"), `dangerfile.js` and `.github/workflows/`; the
+  `astra-review` skill.
+
+## What changed this session
+Find the commit the session started from, and write it in place of `<start>` below. If you cannot, use `origin/main`
+and report "coverage incomplete". If neither exists, report committed changes as not covered, and still inspect staged,
+unstaged and untracked work.
+```bash
+git log --oneline <start>..HEAD           # commits
+git diff <start> HEAD                     # what they changed
+git status --short
+git diff --cached                         # staged: what the next commit holds
+git diff                                  # unstaged
+git ls-files --others --exclude-standard  # untracked: read those that matter
+```
+<!-- Check staged and unstaged separately: `git diff HEAD` nets them out, so a change staged and then undone in the
+working tree shows nothing yet still lands. Add live changes the checks depend on (a deployed build, a grant), read
+with the time. -->
+Run it here and in each orchestrator checkout or worktree the session touched. `<start>` in each is
+`git rev-list -1 --before=<start time in the session id from map.mjs session start> main`. Add the pull requests
+merged since then (`gh pr list --state merged --search "merged:>=<start time>"` in each repository), and what runs
+now (`pnpm service:status` in `~/pro/orchestrator`), read with the time.
+
+## Intent
+Does this change fit the authorised intent in the files above? If not:
+
+> When this session's work and the authorised intent disagree:
+>
+> 1. Decide which it is: a defect in the work, an adaptation within what is already authorised, or a change of
+>    intent nobody has decided.
+> 2. Fix a defect in the work.
+> 3. Go ahead with an adaptation within existing authorisation, and record its reason where that helps. Record an
+>    undecided change of intent as a proposal for Justin in `memory/central-scope.md`, "Waiting"; work that depends
+>    on it waits for the decision.
+> 4. Do not edit `SCOPE.md`, `scope.yaml`, the decisions in `memory/` and `decisions/`, or the orchestrator's README
+>    "Direction", `AGENTS.md` rules or `docs/GLOSSARY.md` to match the work unless Justin has recorded that decision.
+> 5. Correct a document directly only when a recorded decision of Justin's already settles it, and cite that
+>    decision.
+> 6. Before making a description, an implementation and a check agree, establish which of them is wrong. A test or
+>    the code shows what is checked or built; it does not authorise weakening a documented constraint.
+
+(Intent-change rule v2, from entropy-guard.)
+
+## Checks
+- If code changed (`src/`, `config/`, `scripts/` in the orchestrator; `tools/` here): does the README and
+  `AGENTS.md` beside it still describe it?
+- For each state claim changed in `STATE.md`: do its other mentions still agree?
+- If the change alters what Iris-app or an agent reaches, launches, stores or reads credentials from, or which tools
+  or card kinds exist: do the orchestrator's `AGENTS.md` ("Boundaries", "Approval cards") and README (opening,
+  "Credentials", "Boundary") still hold? Search the code with the command below, record the search, and extend
+  `test/architecture.test.ts` where the new reach escapes its patterns.
+- If a setting, environment variable, command, path, tool, file or pinned version was added, renamed or removed:
+  search both repositories' docs, `STATE.md` and `memory/` for the old name and value.
+- For each time or live fact written this session (`STATE.md`, `FRICTION.md`, issue or PR text): was it read from
+  `date`, a file time, GitHub or `pnpm service:status`, and does it say where and when?
+- Did Justin decide something this session? It is in its concern's file under "Decisions", not only in `STATE.md`;
+  what it supersedes is marked; a design report it settles says so in its opening. Learnings and friction go where
+  `AGENTS.md`, "Where a learning goes", and `FRICTION.md` say.
+- Does the change respect each working rule in force in `memory/central-scope.md`, such as an area held back or a
+  grant's end date, and has any ended?
+- For each pull request merged this session: did the full `pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm test` pass on its final head,
+  with the whole log in a file, and were its GitHub checks green? A failed check warns rather than blocks.
+- If a concept was named or renamed: does `docs/GLOSSARY.md` already name it, and does new prose avoid its retired
+  words? While the plain "Iris" question in "Waiting" is open, report new plain "Iris" without changing it.
+- Before reviving an archived report, a retired word or a superseded mechanism, such as the env-file Scope list,
+  check `memory/`, `STATE.md` and the glossary for its supersession.
+- If the session pulled `~/pro/orchestrator` or merged Iris-app work: did a restart card appear within seconds (it
+  needs `git config core.hooksPath` to be `.githooks` there), and is what runs read back with `pnpm service:status`?
+```bash
+# Here, in the lab. Then `node tools/map.mjs session end "$SESSION_ID"`, Justin's rule.
+node tools/map.mjs --check
+# In the orchestrator checkout, when its code changed: CI runs neither. SESSION_ID is from map.mjs session start.
+pnpm typecheck > "/tmp/${SESSION_ID:?}-typecheck.log" 2>&1; pnpm test > "/tmp/${SESSION_ID:?}-test.log" 2>&1
+grep -rnE 'child_process|spawn\(|execFile|fork\(|\.launch\(|playwright|worker_threads|\bfetch\b' src config
+grep -rnE 'node:(dns|net|https?)|WebSocket|process\.env|readScopeCredential' src config
+```
+
+## Repairs
+- One owner per concept: when two places define one concept independently, reduce one to a link. Keep summaries,
+  generated projections, versioned copies and independent tests, and keep them correct.
+- Limit every correction by its evidence. Change only what the evidence settles, and leave open parts visibly open.
+  For a claim about everything of a kind, such as what the system reaches or launches, search the code rather than
+  trusting a document and a test that agree, and record the search. Never change a
+  prescribed boundary because of observed behaviour, and never treat a test or the code as the record: where a
+  description, the code and a check disagree, establish which is wrong first.
+
+## Report
+- Baseline, and whether coverage was complete; what was checked and what was not.
+- Findings caused by this session, judged by the relationship changed, not the file edited: renaming a setting in
+  the code makes an untouched README wrong. Problems that were already there, listed separately.
+- Proposals for Justin; files updated; the next action, written into `STATE.md`.
